#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049653 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050336 :topic/math-academy-id 1278 :topic/title "The Angle Between Two Vectors"}] :topic/id #uuid "0875855d-14c4-5fc3-84ce-551f108cb97d" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105916 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105488 :question/id #uuid "1a24b403-754c-5424-8182-8cad3487deed" :question/math-academy-id "e-15606" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=13$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =13\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Scalar Projection Using Angles and Magnitudes"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105917 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105489 :question/id #uuid "da8a1521-86cc-5747-88a7-e2ec56010c11" :question/math-academy-id "e-15607" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 3,3,-1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 2,-7,0 \\rangle.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-7)+(-1)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-7)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{53}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Scalar Projection Using Components"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105490 :question/id #uuid "4693b28f-df75-574e-8e49-bb89e162b107" :question/math-academy-id "e-15608" :question/problem "Let ![](images/e4/e477b14c9148f4132c747a358e1e99f76d40df85b82c66d6e7dd38581b33913c.png) and ![](images/b1/b17d86e3e395b2fd752f5529090c0a5e4e2fdce17642be5594bee98e8c47fed2.png) Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{c}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Projecting a Vector Onto a Perpendicular Vector"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1285 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376 :topic/math-academy-id 3715 :topic/title "Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049322 :topic/math-academy-id 1810 :topic/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Plane and a Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050394 :topic/math-academy-id 1295 :topic/title "Calculating a Vector Projection"}] :topic/title "Calculating a Scalar Projection"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049660 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048789 :topic/math-academy-id 1530 :topic/title "Graphing Exponential Growth Functions"}] :topic/id #uuid "08e2d71b-c870-531a-9550-54bbf2ed62c2" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087821 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087425 :question/id #uuid "8ab1df94-0c4f-5720-a9b6-939dba0ab9c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-1219" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=4^{x}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y=4^{x}{:}$\n\n![](images/df/dfac014ecedaa03a46048b4729ddfa60e7a3776322d0b9ea19035fbcd24bf85b.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y=4^x-1,$ we take the graph of $y=4^x$ and shift it $1$ unit down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-1.$\n\n![](images/ee/eef6c5dd67c683ccd0826caa9760fa240be973f8debd29b41d463f6184a00620.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Growth Curve"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087822 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087426 :question/id #uuid "65c814f8-df2f-549d-9467-c6dda6890bb3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1220" :question/problem "The graph below shows the curve $y=3^{x}+c$ . What is the value of $c?$\n\n![](images/00/00264eb90dbaa2c50fdd626d61292c0428fd0bbbd03a6d051353598450b4ad89.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y=3^x$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y=3^x+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$ Therefore, $c=2.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087427 :question/id #uuid "f73207f4-4661-55f1-acfb-cc5fe6ba9316" :question/math-academy-id "e-1221" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y=3^{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/1c/1cd695e6f1fab35a692fde9003d452862db33a894c23657eb87f8d773de8cbb9.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $5.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c92ea5f3cbfd40bd0dc384b152f6166c27400dbedd93a9c8333195950e7f9c3.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,5)$ to $(0,2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8e8cdccd58a9f1f21c8c2bf0a4a8c08e9c263c4fe91cc92e2bee334de057116.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087428 :question/id #uuid "62f7d6e1-61ec-5f3d-8cdd-4da0c90c7ccf" :question/math-academy-id "e-1222" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=k(6^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](images/de/deb7e20658882229a3653f4058c787bf2a5c4a26c5f2982406a2dba34c2d5846.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k(6^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4$, so $c=4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k(6^{x})+4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({6}^{x})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k({6}^{0})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k(1)+4 \\\\ 6 & =k+4 \\\\ 2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=2(6^{x})+4.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :topic/math-academy-id 817 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049379 :topic/math-academy-id 456 :topic/title "Vertical Translations of Exponential Decay Functions"}] :topic/title "Vertical Translations of Exponential Growth Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049672 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699 :topic/math-academy-id 849 :topic/title "Equations of Ellipses Centered at a General Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050722 :topic/math-academy-id 806 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Circles"}] :topic/id #uuid "09a480ff-86b3-5a9f-87fc-9be4a100ee60" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152187 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151989 :question/id #uuid "e57f9dab-c5db-55d9-9b21-375bbc92281c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6814" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](images/5d/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152188 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151990 :question/id #uuid "8f1ee01d-29ac-5fb8-a5c9-e0995afa269c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6815" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152189 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151991 :question/id #uuid "d97dc879-9840-5531-8247-5229e5eb8bb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6816" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](images/47/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152190 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151992 :question/id #uuid "9abded65-4a6e-5ebc-b496-d61b28179288" :question/math-academy-id "e-6818" :question/problem "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](images/6c/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/a6/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2746 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048450 :topic/math-academy-id 1947 :topic/title "Calculating Global Extrema of Multivariable Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049209 :topic/math-academy-id 3241 :topic/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Complex Eigenvalues"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049482 :topic/math-academy-id 3596 :topic/title "Parametrizations of Cylinders"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050104 :topic/math-academy-id 1401 :topic/title "Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vectors in 2D"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050616 :topic/math-academy-id 3690 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Ellipses"}] :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Ellipses"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049715 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048882 :topic/math-academy-id 1101 :topic/title "The Magnitude of a Vector"}] :topic/id #uuid "0d3ad89a-14be-5528-a12e-4252d281243e" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096445 :question/id #uuid "069b2882-f4a9-589f-a903-4e89d077cd85" :question/math-academy-id "e-4279" :question/problem "Suppose, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$. Given that $7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\lambda \\left(3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b} \\right)$ are equal vectors, find the scalar factor $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\n7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}= \\dfrac{7}{3} (3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Vectors are Parallel"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096649 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096446 :question/id #uuid "d1329498-4413-5d87-a814-be0d57a3afd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4280" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf a$ is shown in the diagram below. Draw the vectors $2\\mathbf a$ and $-3\\mathbf a$.\n\n![](images/a5/a5eaa8baaf303ecbfa6c0fb207d6b19836cc90fc331e238cd473109f44fddf80.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ must be parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2\\mathbf{a}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $2\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\n![](images/41/41eba7e6c5bbe420418726bd45efa668dc6be5ff5144ba35d52b53a43925c333.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $-3\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3\\mathbf{a}$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-3$ is negative.\n\n![](images/ed/eda3f2572d98ef1789292ec6d35569487b32efa459673dcb95f8fdd0d1f4ae5b.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Creating Parallel Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096494 :question/id #uuid "11c09a26-0e83-5a77-b777-aea8a3594960" :question/math-academy-id "e-5183" :question/problem "Consider the diagram that is shown below. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{c}$\n2. $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$\n3. $\\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{e}$\n\n![](images/95/95ae711c1ccbf7543e6d269aa8d817a66e2ac348113ea71f3a1babcbb23d1ca3.png)" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. The vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ have different magnitudes. So, $\\mathbf{b} \\neq -\\mathbf{c}$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{e}$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Statement III is false. The magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is greater than the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq 2\\mathbf{e}$.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1103 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049360 :topic/math-academy-id 1104 :topic/title "Unit Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051014 :topic/math-academy-id 1105 :topic/title "Linear Combinations of Vectors and Their Properties"}] :topic/title "Parallel Vectors"}]}
